  "name": "Material Aging and Temporal Depth \u2014 Patina as Perceptual Enrichment",
  "structural_pattern": "Buildings change over time. Natural materials (stone, wood, copper) tend to age 'positively' by increasing in fractal complexity (T1) and encoding temporal information (patina). Synthetic materials often age 'negatively' by degrading without gaining complexity. Occupants perceive this 'temporal depth' as a signal of continuity, care, and embodied time. CALIBRATION (TP-II): Trajectory bifurcation: Natural=Convergent to D~1.3; Synthetic=Divergent (Entropy).",
  "higher_order_principle": "Temporal depth is a perceptual quality. Architecture creates a relationship with time through the weathering characteristics of its materials. Positive aging aligns with fractal adaptation; negative aging aligns with entropy/disorder. MATERIALS MUST BE SELECTED FOR THEIR AGING PROPERTIES, NOT JUST INITIAL STATE.",
  "transferable_to": [
    "Product Design (heirloom quality)",
    "Landscape Architecture (seasonal succession)",
    "Urban Conservation"
  ],
